1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,373
You just ran git commit, and the second
it finished,

2
00:00:03,393 --> 00:00:04,820
you realised it was wrong.

3
00:00:05,190 --> 00:00:07,773
Maybe it's the wrong file. Maybe it's
the wrong

4
00:00:07,793 --> 00:00:09,656
branch. Maybe it's already pushed.

5
00:00:10,026 --> 00:00:14,474
Git has four tools for this: restore,
reset, revert, and reflog.

6
00:00:14,844 --> 00:00:19,165
They look similar, but they do very
different things. Pick the wrong one,

7
00:00:19,185 --> 00:00:22,912
and you can lose work, or rewrite
history your team depends on.

8
00:00:24,033 --> 00:00:27,605
Every Git mistake becomes easier once
you ask one question:

9
00:00:27,625 --> 00:00:28,822
which place changed?

10
00:00:29,192 --> 00:00:33,629
There are three. The working directory,
where your files are. The index,

11
00:00:33,649 --> 00:00:36,663
also called the staging area. And the
repository,

12
00:00:36,683 --> 00:00:38,210
where commits are stored.

13
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:40,993
HEAD points to the commit you are
currently on.

14
00:00:41,363 --> 00:00:45,541
Each undo command touches a different
combination of these places.

15
00:00:45,911 --> 00:00:47,467
Keep that picture in mind,

16
00:00:47,487 --> 00:00:49,891
because every command that follows moves

17
00:00:49,911 --> 00:00:52,133
something between these three places.

18
00:00:53,267 --> 00:00:55,861
Start with the smallest tool: git
restore.

19
00:00:56,231 --> 00:00:59,492
If you edited a file and want it back
the way it was,

20
00:00:59,512 --> 00:01:01,473
git restore, then the file name,

21
00:01:01,493 --> 00:01:04,445
discards that change from the working
directory.

22
00:01:04,815 --> 00:01:06,456
If you already ran git add,

23
00:01:06,476 --> 00:01:10,762
git restore with the staged flag moves
the file back out of the index,

24
00:01:10,782 --> 00:01:11,993
but keeps your edit.

25
00:01:12,363 --> 00:01:17,131
Restore never touches your commits. It
only fixes the files and the index.

26
00:01:18,267 --> 00:01:21,968
Now say you made a commit and want to
undo it. That's git reset.

27
00:01:22,338 --> 00:01:25,681
Reset moves your branch back to an
earlier commit. What

28
00:01:25,701 --> 00:01:28,372
happens to your changes depends on the
mode.

29
00:01:28,742 --> 00:01:32,215
With soft, the branch moves, but your
changes stay staged,

30
00:01:32,235 --> 00:01:33,540
ready to commit again.

31
00:01:33,910 --> 00:01:37,904
With mixed, the default, the branch
moves and the index resets,

32
00:01:37,924 --> 00:01:41,026
so your changes stay in your files, but
unstaged.

33
00:01:41,396 --> 00:01:44,713
With hard, everything resets: the
branch, the index,

34
00:01:44,733 --> 00:01:48,885
and your files. Your changes are gone
from the working directory.

35
00:01:49,255 --> 00:01:53,356
One more detail: hard reset does not
delete untracked files.

36
00:01:54,500 --> 00:01:58,197
Reset is fine for commits that only
exist on your machine.

37
00:01:58,567 --> 00:02:01,781
But if the bad commit is already pushed
to a shared branch,

38
00:02:01,801 --> 00:02:03,042
do not rewrite history.

39
00:02:03,412 --> 00:02:06,343
Use git revert. It creates a brand new
commit

40
00:02:06,363 --> 00:02:08,507
that applies the opposite change.

41
00:02:08,877 --> 00:02:11,487
The original bad commit stays in
history,

42
00:02:11,507 --> 00:02:16,171
so everyone's copy still lines up, and
nobody has to repair their branch.

43
00:02:17,300 --> 00:02:19,705
Now the scary one. You ran reset --hard,

44
00:02:19,725 --> 00:02:22,190
and your commit disappeared from the
log.

45
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:26,418
It isn't gone. Git keeps a local journal
of where HEAD has been,

46
00:02:26,438 --> 00:02:27,509
called the reflog.

47
00:02:27,879 --> 00:02:31,118
Run git reflog, find the line for your
lost commit,

48
00:02:31,138 --> 00:02:32,907
and reset --hard back to it.

49
00:02:33,277 --> 00:02:37,118
The reflog is local, and old entries
expire eventually,

50
00:02:37,138 --> 00:02:38,452
so recover quickly.

51
00:02:39,600 --> 00:02:43,832
Let's replay it in a throwaway
repository. Three commits:

52
00:02:43,852 --> 00:02:46,518
A, B, and C, where C is the mistake.

53
00:02:46,888 --> 00:02:51,165
First, git reset soft, HEAD tilde one.
The log now ends at B,

54
00:02:51,185 --> 00:02:53,912
and git status shows the change staged,

55
00:02:53,932 --> 00:02:56,589
with the letter M in the first column.

56
00:02:56,959 --> 00:03:01,723
Start over, and run git reset mixed
instead. Same commit history,

57
00:03:01,743 --> 00:03:07,391
but now the M sits in the second column.
The change is in the file, unstaged.

58
00:03:07,761 --> 00:03:11,600
Start over, and run git reset --hard.
Git status is empty,

59
00:03:11,620 --> 00:03:14,462
and the file is back to how it looked at
B.

60
00:03:14,832 --> 00:03:19,067
Now the rescue. Git reflog shows the
reset, and right below it,

61
00:03:19,087 --> 00:03:20,823
the commit called C, oops.

62
00:03:21,193 --> 00:03:22,965
reset --hard to HEAD at one,

63
00:03:22,985 --> 00:03:27,124
in braces, and the file is back. That is
the safety net, working.

64
00:03:28,267 --> 00:03:30,241
Three mistakes show up again and again.

65
00:03:30,611 --> 00:03:33,592
One: resetting a commit that is already
pushed,

66
00:03:33,612 --> 00:03:36,339
then force-pushing. Teammates' branches
now

67
00:03:36,359 --> 00:03:39,149
disagree with yours. If you must force-
push,

68
00:03:39,169 --> 00:03:42,087
use force with lease, and tell the team
first.

69
00:03:42,457 --> 00:03:45,287
Two: running reset --hard with edits you
never

70
00:03:45,307 --> 00:03:47,580
committed. The reflog tracks commits,

71
00:03:47,600 --> 00:03:51,609
so changes that were never committed are
generally gone for good.

72
00:03:51,979 --> 00:03:55,006
Three: believing revert removes a
secret. It

73
00:03:55,026 --> 00:03:57,846
doesn't. The old commit stays in
history,

74
00:03:57,866 --> 00:04:02,072
so rotate the credential first, then
clean history if needed.

75
00:04:03,200 --> 00:04:07,346
Interviewers rarely ask for command
names. They ask for scenarios.

76
00:04:07,716 --> 00:04:10,935
For example: a faulty commit is on
protected main,

77
00:04:10,955 --> 00:04:12,749
and it has already deployed.

78
00:04:13,119 --> 00:04:18,009
A strong answer follows a framework:
impact, evidence, investigation,

79
00:04:18,029 --> 00:04:22,776
immediate mitigation, communication,
permanent fix, and prevention.

80
00:04:23,146 --> 00:04:25,538
Here, the mitigation is git revert,

81
00:04:25,558 --> 00:04:29,742
because main is shared. The permanent
fix is a tested change,

82
00:04:29,762 --> 00:04:33,601
and prevention is branch protection and
required checks.

83
00:04:33,971 --> 00:04:38,211
Notice the order: stabilise production
first, then investigate,

84
00:04:38,231 --> 00:04:40,104
and only then fix the cause.

85
00:04:41,233 --> 00:04:44,447
Reading is not enough. Practise in a
safe sandbox.

86
00:04:44,817 --> 00:04:49,641
The Git Command Master Map repository
has an undo and reflog lab,

87
00:04:49,661 --> 00:04:54,410
where every destructive command runs in
a disposable repository.

88
00:04:54,780 --> 00:04:56,477
The link is in the description.

89
00:04:56,847 --> 00:05:01,616
Recap: restore fixes files. Reset moves
your branch. Revert undoes

90
00:05:01,636 --> 00:05:05,824
safely on shared branches. Reflog saves
you when you slip.

91
00:05:06,194 --> 00:05:09,771
Subscribe, and tell me which Git
scenario to visualize next.

92
00:05:10,141 --> 00:05:13,637
The labs are safe to break, so run each
command twice:

93
00:05:13,657 --> 00:05:16,957
once to predict the result, and once to
confirm it.

